```java
import java.sql.*;

public class GoodExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String url = "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/mydb";
        String user = "root";
        String password = "password";

        try (Connection conn = DriverManager.getConnection(url, user, password)) {
            String sql = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = ?";
            PreparedStatement stmt = conn.prepareStatement(sql);
            stmt.setString(1, username);
            ResultSet rs = stmt.executeQuery();
            while (rs.next()) {
                System.out.println(rs.getString("username"));
            }
        } catch (SQLException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

In the bad code snippet, the SQL query is constructed by concatenating the user-provided username directly into the query string. This is vulnerable because it allows an attacker to inject arbitrary SQL code, potentially leading to SQL injection attacks.

In the good code snippet, a prepared statement is used instead. The SQL query is constructed with a placeholder (`?`) for the user-provided username. The actual value is then set using the `setString` method of the `PreparedStatement` object. This ensures that the user-provided value is treated as a parameter and not as part of the SQL query, thus preventing SQL injection attacks.
